{"name":"Cosy Nook","hex":"#fba765","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cosy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Affording comfort and warmth; snug; social and comfortable.","Warm and comfortable."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A padded or knit covering put on an item to keep it warm, especially a teapot or egg.","A padded or knit covering for any item (often an electronic device such as a laptop computer)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cosy"},{"term":"nook","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small corner formed by two walls; an alcove.","A hidden or secluded spot; a secluded retreat."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To withdraw into a nook.","To situate in a nook."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nook"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barnes & Noble Nook","blocks":[{"text":"The Barnes & Noble Nook is a brand of e-readers developed by American book retailer Barnes & Noble, based on the Android platform. The original device was announced in the U.S. in October 2009, and was released the next month. The original Nook had a six-inch e-paper display and a separate, smaller color touchscreen that serves as the primary input device and was capable of Wi-Fi and AT&T 3G wireless connectivity. The original Nook was followed in November 2010 by a color LCD device called the Nook Color, in June 2011 by the Nook Simple Touch, and in November 2011 and February 2012 by the Nook Tablet. On April 30, 2012, Barnes & Noble entered into a partnership with Microsoft that spun off the Nook and college businesses into a subsidiary. On August 28, 2012, Barnes & Noble announced partnerships with retailers in the UK, which began offering the Nook digital products in October 2012. In December 2014, B&N purchased Microsoft's Nook shares, ending the partnership."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/B%26N_nook_Logo.svg/330px-B%26N_nook_Logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c